          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A farmer presents with jaundice, renal failure, and haemorrhagic manifestations </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A farmer presents with jaundice, renal failure, and haemorrhagic manifestations after wading through flood water. To confirm the infecting serovar of Leptospira, which investigation should be requested?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>IgM ELISA</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>IgM ELISA is a rapid screening test for leptospirosis but cannot identify the specific serovar — it detects genus-level antibodies only.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Dark-field microscopy of blood</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Dark-field microscopy can visualise spirochaetes but is insensitive and prone to artefacts; it does not determine the serovar.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Microscopic Agglutination Test (MAT)</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>MAT is the gold standard serology for leptospirosis. It uses panels of live serovar antigens, so a rising titre identifies the specific infecting serovar.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Blood culture in EMJH medium</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Culture in EMJH medium isolates Leptospira in the first 7–10 days but takes weeks to grow and is not used for serovar confirmation in clinical practice.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The Microscopic Agglutination Test (MAT) is the gold standard. It uses panels of live serovar antigens, enabling both diagnosis and serovar identification — critical for epidemiological tracking in endemic areas."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! MAT detects serovar-specific antibodies against live leptospiral antigens — it is the reference standard for both confirming leptospirosis and identifying the serovar."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
