          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which opportunistic infection becomes a significant risk specifically when an HI</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which opportunistic infection becomes a significant risk specifically when an HIV-positive patient's CD4 count falls below 100 cells/μL?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Oral candidiasis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Oral candidiasis occurs at a higher CD4 threshold (&lt;500 cells/μL) and is one of the earliest opportunistic infections in HIV progression.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PCP)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>PCP typically manifests when the CD4 count drops below 200 cells/μL — this is why co-trimoxazole prophylaxis is started at that threshold.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cryptococcal meningitis</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Cryptococcus neoformans causes subacute meningitis at CD4 &lt;100 cells/μL. It is detected by India ink preparation (capsulated yeast) or the rapid CrAg lateral flow assay.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Toxoplasma encephalitis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Toxoplasma encephalitis occurs at CD4 &lt;200 cells/μL, the same threshold as PCP, and presents with ring-enhancing lesions on imaging.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Cryptococcal meningitis is the key OI at CD4 <100. The thresholds to remember: <500 (candidiasis, herpes zoster), <200 (PCP, toxoplasmosis), <100 (cryptococcus, CMV retinitis), <50 (MAC, CMV colitis)."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Cryptococcal meningitis characteristically appears at CD4 <100 cells/μL — remembering these thresholds guides both prophylaxis decisions and clinical suspicion."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
