          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In HPV-associated cervical carcinogenesis, oncoprotein E6 drives malignant trans</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In HPV-associated cervical carcinogenesis, oncoprotein E6 drives malignant transformation by targeting which tumour suppressor protein for degradation?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Rb (retinoblastoma protein)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Rb is the target of HPV oncoprotein E7, not E6. E7 inactivates Rb, releasing E2F transcription factor to drive uncontrolled cell cycle entry.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>p53</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>E6 binds and degrades p53, the 'guardian of the genome', thereby preventing apoptosis of HPV-infected cells and allowing accumulation of oncogenic mutations.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>BRCA1</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>BRCA1 is a tumour suppressor involved in DNA double-strand break repair and is associated with hereditary breast and ovarian cancers — it is not a target of HPV oncoproteins.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>E2F transcription factor</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>E2F is not degraded — it is actually released when E7 inactivates Rb, which then promotes uncontrolled cell cycle progression.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "HPV oncoprotein E6 binds and degrades p53, preventing apoptosis. Remember the pair: E6 → p53 degradation, E7 → Rb inactivation. Both are needed for the persistent proliferation that leads to CIN and invasive carcinoma."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! E6 degrades p53 (preventing apoptosis) while E7 inactivates Rb (releasing E2F) — together they are the two-hit mechanism driving HPV-associated cervical carcinogenesis over 10–20 years."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
